#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173013} :activity/id #uuid "1b7424bb-16f3-51a5-8f12-862fc36f5a2a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050694 :topic/id #uuid "5f79a517-5af5-5b02-b387-db32653ca27a" :topic/math-academy-id 3361} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172099 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171905 :question/id #uuid "1c03777d-2ead-5333-bf75-0e9a96fdb0e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9563"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "629dd9d1-1c53-5a04-97bf-5799e71b9573" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481405}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Other Type I and Type II Regions"} :step/id #uuid "ff8c90c5-7280-517d-b629-b9fcf49eb545" :step/math-academy-id 35835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172100 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171906 :question/id #uuid "a9d748d6-d688-5b77-a250-cb534a371765" :question/math-academy-id "e-9564"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5c7e707-c978-566d-8a69-98c84c7eb53e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051070}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549299} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424524}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem in Flux Form"} :step/id #uuid "176aba1b-a4f1-53bf-b585-f21efd3b5ea7" :step/math-academy-id 35836 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173011}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173011 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172101 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171907 :question/id #uuid "ed1dfb37-d01e-5d35-82f2-a441cdd54f3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9565"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a418f9-b4da-53b4-a39c-75d28dfa8595" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520745} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541081}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across Rectangles"} :step/id #uuid "b533c8b3-90f2-54ee-ae35-a98929d6ba6e" :step/math-academy-id 35837 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173009}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173012 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173207 :tutorial/content "There is some nice intuition behind the flux form of Green's theorem that makes it easier to remember.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with component functions $P$ and $Q$ defined over a region $D.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem in flux form states that the sum of the microscopic \"fluxes\" (divergences) of $\\mathbf F$ inside $D$ equals the macroscopic flux of $\\mathbf F$ across the boundary of $D.$\n\n![](images/47/47a81125069be94b4ccda6f228ff1b8253f99595c6f3c4ae0ca710bdb796215f.png)\n\n    \n\nRecall that the divergence (i.e., the microscopic flux) of $\\mathbf F$ at a point is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nIf we sum (integrate) the divergence at every point over the entire domain $D,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y} \\right)\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThis expression gives us one side of Green's theorem in flux form.\n\nThe macroscopic divergence is simply the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across the boundary of $D{:}$\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\mathbf n \\, \\textrm d s\n$$\n\nEquating the sum of the divergences with the flux, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y} \\right)\\textrm d A = \\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\mathbf n \\, \\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nThe flux form of Green's theorem can be summarized in a single picture, as follows:\n\n![](images/0f/0f8ba4b7e853e3b59e8677b0954c3cfae1b9c482750e13a742bd4d45eda8005d.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "9016229b-93aa-58fc-857c-2a2d3f337ca4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5862 :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind Green's Theorem in Flux Form"} :step/id #uuid "e5c29654-0b83-51e0-a3d0-c6fbf08c8f2d" :step/math-academy-id 35877 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173010}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173013 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173208 :tutorial/content "Green's theorem allows us to write line integrals as double integrals, and we've seen how Green's theorem simplifies circulation calculations. Even more, Green's theorem can also be used to simplify flux calculations!\n\nTo see how, we first recall that the flux of $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) =  P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\mathbf j$ across $C$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s= \\oint\\limits_C P\\, \\textrm{d}y -Q\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is closed and $\\mathbf n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nNow, let's set $M= -Q$ and $N=P$. Then, by the circulation form of Green's theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x & ={∮}_{C}N\\,\\text{d}y+M\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}M\\,\\text{d}x+N\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂N}{∂x}-\\frac{∂M}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}-(-\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}))\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we've obtained the **Green's theorem in flux form**, which we restate below:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} +\\dfrac{\\partial  Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F =  \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can also write Green's theorem in flux form as\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem in flux form allows us to convert a flux line integral into an equivalent double integral." :tutorial/id #uuid "7f3b4b70-8b58-53d1-bc8d-a42daddfab4d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5868 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "528120c2-edf7-5b3d-a8d3-0b481b460d90" :step/math-academy-id 35900 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173012}}] :activity/title "Green's Theorem in Flux Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172181}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481444} :activity/id #uuid "fc8f6cbb-80b9-555e-be16-0b6e2e481fd7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050694 :topic/id #uuid "5f79a517-5af5-5b02-b387-db32653ca27a" :topic/math-academy-id 3361} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481444 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cb0875b-4ef8-58eb-8217-9c53fb18ec9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-5y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260529} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8d79b2-c27e-5bcf-9329-f7f7ae12d537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+5y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261120} {:answer/id #uuid "9f228826-f550-52dc-83d1-70ff2e48a178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261255} {:answer/id #uuid "c8cc4bc7-93b1-5f30-8801-7d45f0f67ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261649} {:answer/id #uuid "dd26827a-6b30-5805-9ec2-35c779e0f6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}x(2y-5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261831}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261649} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1a2ac70-d4cf-5928-8804-e1eb884d3787" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bab01488-68e9-5dde-9b92-c15b0288a3e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-127072" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i+5xy\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/265d27ad87a7f114bd2a32a5f48cbfedb13192abf48f9b759c4ed635e71ff144.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=5xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+5x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2y+5)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1736de57-90cb-505e-97a3-07df8bb92125" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481473}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481472 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262736 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4a4e6e-8fd1-5522-991a-091f0cb886bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{y}^{2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260102} {:answer/id #uuid "20290a0e-d346-5c30-942c-4f76e1fbd4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(6{y}^{2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260151} {:answer/id #uuid "a3837ae4-763a-595f-890a-8cc4ef3801c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{3}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261299} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b24ec3-56ed-5096-95a0-c3abce767ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{3}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261495} {:answer/id #uuid "fa68bf59-8089-510b-855b-d18f12a481e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{y}^{2}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262064}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261299} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e39b9c2-8ba1-5d6a-8730-4f02deaa62b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64b95036-f7c8-5305-8593-d176862393c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-127229" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0),(1,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)=3x{y}^{3}\\,i+6x{y}^{4}\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa98f3db31860a463d0a551d5297895e61a6c041d852c04de04c44a194510961.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3x{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=6x{y}^{4}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =24x{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{y}^{3}+24x{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{3}(1+8x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e6fdd744-d51d-5e7d-83a1-1dc38e3a78f6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481474}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481473 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387803 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a2f6f5b-f063-50fd-a445-e5206861484d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2{y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387805} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3a5b14-e539-50ca-b764-e8483d96d0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}2{y}^{2}(x+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387806} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2802b1-16eb-55bd-a33e-9c02fc8a176a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x({y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387807} {:answer/id #uuid "8051d521-18c9-5506-8dd8-4404b731cd23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}+12y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387808} {:answer/id #uuid "2d6e2d0e-132f-5112-af04-61afe3543d74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387809}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387807} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7357b1f-7672-551e-bf04-066c16895432" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6b4bd86f-3707-5569-a669-abddd19a5594" :question/math-academy-id "q-127228" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(3,0),(3,2),$ and $(0,2),$  traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,i+12xy\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/23/23066b23aebf332921c22a0439a414ade8dffd2a270641d186da0ed0e9488821.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=12xy.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =12x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x{y}^{2}+12x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x({y}^{2}+6)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e7a03c0e-bfb8-569b-b911-e9045812e018" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481472}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481474 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481405 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35a88b30-d359-5319-ad67-03c9ec1379b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480844} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3727fe-7ec5-539d-8680-0d97034aab9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480857} {:answer/id #uuid "5f0f5910-9dd0-5dfd-8b84-640bae613ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480916} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ac67f8-eb09-591a-819e-dd06587385a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481081} {:answer/id #uuid "d822e6cb-6ef9-5fe7-90de-d07cd210051f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481143}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481143} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1420a1f-e109-58bc-a6f7-ed1ec23a0ca0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9ae57b7-ac91-5f05-88c4-1e8e0aed8174" :question/math-academy-id "q-127318" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨x+\\sqrt{y},\\,y+\\sqrt{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and the line $y=0$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e5/e570374b37c116e32c3a7762b8cf09a28bdbb3e06b5e67e25837902a89bcd264.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=y+\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ec17ec80-3152-54be-96ed-dd7762345db6"}] :activity/title "Green's Theorem in Flux Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480737}]}
